            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p><strong>Soil</strong> is the thin upper layer of the Earth's crust that supports plant life, formed by the protracted mechanical disintegration and chemical decomposition of parent rocks under the influence of climate, vegetation, relief, and time.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Master Soil Horizons (The Soil Profile)</h4>\n            <p>A vertical cross-section through a mature, well-differentiated soil reveals distinctive horizontal layers termed <strong>master horizons</strong>:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>O Horizon (Organic Layer):</strong> The uppermost surface layer composed of fresh, decomposing, or humified leaf litter, twigs, and organic debris. Prominent in forest soils; absent in plowed agricultural soils.</li>\n                <li><strong>A Horizon (Topsoil):</strong> Mineral horizon rich in decomposed organic matter (humus). It is the zone of maximum biological activity (microbes, earthworms, plant root density). Usually dark in color.</li>\n                <li><strong>E Horizon (Zone of Eluviation):</strong> A pale, bleached layer directly beneath A, where organic acids and percolating water have leached out (eluviated) silicate clay, iron, and aluminum oxides.</li>\n                <li><strong>B Horizon (Subsoil / Zone of Illuviation):</strong> Deposition layer where clays, iron, and aluminum leached from upper horizons accumulate. Possesses dense blocky or prismatic structure.</li>\n                <li><strong>C Horizon (Regolith / Parent Material):</strong> Partially weathered, unconsolidated rock fragments representing the parent mineral stock; practically devoid of biological humus.</li>\n                <li><strong>R Horizon (Bedrock):</strong> The unweathered, solid, impenetrable consolidated bedrock (e.g., granite, basalt, sandstone).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. ICAR Soil Classification System (1953)</h4>\n            <p>The <strong>Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR)</strong>, based on pedological genesis, color, and geographic distribution, divides Indian soils into <strong>eight major soil orders</strong>:</p>\n            <ol>\n                <li><strong>Alluvial Soils</strong> (~40% of total area)</li>\n                <li><strong>Black Soils (Regur)</strong> (~15% of total area)</li>\n                <li><strong>Red and Yellow Soils</strong> (~18.5% of total area)</li>\n                <li><strong>Laterite Soils</strong> (~4.3% of total area)</li>\n                <li><strong>Arid and Desert Soils</strong> (~4.3% of total area)</li>\n                <li><strong>Saline and Alkaline Soils (Usar/Reh)</strong></li>\n                <li><strong>Peaty and Organic Soils (Kari)</strong></li>\n                <li><strong>Forest and Mountain Soils</strong> (~8% of total area)</li>\n            </ol>\n\n            <h4>3. Modern USDA Soil Taxonomy Orders in India</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>USDA Soil Order</th><th>Percentage of Indian Land Area</th><th>Key Diagnostic Feature & Indian Soil Equivalent</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Inceptisols</strong></td><td><strong>39.7% (Largest in India)</strong></td><td>Embryonic soils with weakly developed B-horizons; mature Gangetic alluvium.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Entisols</strong></td><td><strong>28.1% (Second largest)</strong></td><td>Youthful, recently deposited soils with no diagnostic horizons (Khadar silt, desert dunes).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Alfisols</strong></td><td><strong>13.6%</strong></td><td>Naturally fertile leached soils with clay-enriched subsoil; Red soils of southern India.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Vertisols</strong></td><td><strong>8.5%</strong></td><td>High-clay soils that shrink, crack, and swell (self-inverting); Black Cotton Soils (Regur).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Aridisols</strong></td><td><strong>4.3%</strong></td><td>Desert soils with low organic content, carbonate/salt pans; Thar Desert soils.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Ultisols</strong></td><td><strong>2.5%</strong></td><td>Intensely weathered acidic leached red-yellow soils of humid tropics; Laterites.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Mollisols</strong></td><td><strong>0.4%</strong></td><td>Thick, dark, soft grassland soils rich in base cations; Himalayan forest soils.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
