            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p><strong>Alluvial Soils</strong> constitute the single largest, most productive, and economically vital soil group of India, covering approximately <strong>$143\text{ million hectares}$ (nearly $43\\%$ of the country's total land area)</strong>. They anchor the breadbasket of the nation, sustaining over $50\\%$ of India's population.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Genesis & Spatial Extent</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Azonal / Transported Nature:</strong> Unlike sedentary (in-situ) soils, alluvial soils are <em>transported (ex-situ)</em> soils formed by the mechanical deposition of massive riverine silt loads (alluvium) derived from the erosion of the Himalayas and the northern fringes of the Peninsular plateau.</li>\n                <li><strong>Spatial Distribution:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Continuously covers the vast <strong>Indo-Gangetic-Brahmaputra Plains</strong> across Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, and Assam.</li>\n                        <li>Extends through a narrow corridor in Rajasthan into the plains of Gujarat (Narmada, Tapti, Mahi, Sabarmati).</li>\n                        <li>Occurs along the eastern coastal littoral in the deltas of the <strong>Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, and Kaveri</strong> (coastal alluvium).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Morphological Dichotomy: Bhangar vs Khadar</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Bhangar (Older Alluvium)</th><th>Khadar (Newer Alluvium)</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Topographic Position</strong></td><td>Forms elevated river terraces situated <strong>well above modern flood levels</strong>.</td><td>Occurs in the <strong>active low-lying floodplains</strong> and river channels.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Age of Formation</strong></td><td>Deposited during the Middle to Late <strong>Pleistocene epoch</strong>.</td><td>Deposited during the <strong>Holocene (Recent) epoch</strong>; replenished annually.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Texture & Color</strong></td><td>Clayey to compact loam; darker yellowish-brown color.</td><td>Porous sandy loam and fine silt; light pale grey color.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Calcareous Inclusions</strong></td><td>Contains impure nodular concretions of calcium carbonate termed <strong>Kankar</strong>.</td><td>Non-calcareous; free from kankar nodules; highly permeable.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Inherent Fertility</strong></td><td>Moderately fertile; requires heavy chemical manuring and deep irrigation.</td><td><strong>Supreme natural fertility</strong>; constantly recharged with fresh nutrient-rich mountain silt.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>3. Chemical Composition & Nutrient Profile</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Adequate / Rich Elements:</strong> High in <strong>Potash (K₂O)</strong> and <strong>Lime (Calcium carbonate, CaO)</strong>; contains adequate iron and alumina.</li>\n                <li><strong>Acutely Deficient Elements:</strong> Universally poor in <strong>Nitrogen (N)</strong>, <strong>Phosphorus (P₂O₅)</strong>, and <strong>Humus (organic carbon)</strong> due to intense tropical microbial decomposition and rapid mineralization.</li>\n                <li><strong>Agronomic Response:</strong> Displays high chemical responsiveness to synthetic NPK chemical fertilizers, organic compost, and canal/tubewell irrigation, yielding massive harvests of wheat, paddy, sugarcane, jute, oilseeds, and vegetables.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
