            "id": "m03",
            "title": "Black Soils (Regur): Basalt Genesis, Montmorillonite & Self-Ploughing",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p><strong>Black Soil</strong>, known locally as <strong>Regur</strong> (from the Telugu word <em>'Reguda'</em>), is one of the most distinctive and pedologically fascinating soil orders of India. Covering approximately <strong>$50\text{ to }55\text{ million hectares}$ (about $16\\%$ of India's total geographical area)</strong>, it is the agricultural backbone of western and central Peninsular India.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Parent Rock & Pedogenesis</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Sedentary (In-Situ) Nature:</strong> Unlike transported alluvium, black soil is a <strong>residual (in-situ) soil</strong> formed directly by the prolonged sub-tropical weathering of the horizontal basaltic lava sheets of the <strong>Deccan Traps</strong> (erupted $\\sim 66\text{ mya}$).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Black Color Paradox:</strong> The dark black-to-chestnut color is <em>not</em> due to organic humus (which is very low), but to the presence of small concentrations of <strong>titaniferous magnetite</strong>, complex iron-titanium silicates, and colloidal clay-organic complexes.</li>\n                <li><strong>Geographical Spread:</strong> Dominates <strong>Maharashtra</strong> (occupying nearly the entire state except coastal Konkan), extending into the <strong>Kathiawar and Kutch peninsulas of Gujarat</strong>, the <strong>Malwa Plateau and Narmada valley of Madhya Pradesh</strong>, the <strong>Deccan fringe of northern Karnataka</strong> (Bijapur, Dharwar, Belgaum), and western Telangana.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Montmorillonite Mineralogy & 'Self-Ploughing' Property</h4>\n            <p>Black soil contains a high clay fraction (often exceeding <strong>$50\\%–60\\%$</strong>), dominated mineralogically by <strong>Montmorillonite</strong> (a 2:1 expanding crystal lattice smectite clay). This gives rise to unique physical behaviors:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Extraordinary Moisture Retentiveness:</strong> Black soil absorbs moisture slowly but holds it with immense capillary tenacity. It retains moisture deep in the subsoil long after rains cease, allowing dryland winter crops (rabi jowar, wheat, safflower) to thrive without irrigation.</li>\n                <li><strong>The 'Self-Ploughing' (Self-Mulching) Mechanism:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>During the scorching pre-monsoon summer, severe moisture loss causes the montmorillonite clay to contract violently, developing <strong>wide, gaping vertical fissures (cracks up to $1\text{ meter deep and }10–15\text{ cm wide}$)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Crumbled dry crumbs of surface topsoil fall down into these gaping fissures.</li>\n                        <li>When the monsoon rains arrive, the soil swells rapidly, hydrating and closing the cracks. This cyclical churning produces a continuous natural turnover and deep aeration of the soil profile, famously designated as <strong>'Self-Ploughing'</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Extreme Stickiness:</strong> When saturated, the soil swells into a gummy, cohesive, plastic paste that sticks tenaciously to plowshares and animal hooves, rendering plowing impossible. Consequently, farmers must plow fields immediately after the <strong>very first pre-monsoon rain</strong> before full saturation occurs.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Chemical Profile & Crop Adaptability</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Nutrient Richness:</strong> Abundant in <strong>Lime (CaO)</strong>, <strong>Iron</strong>, <strong>Magnesium (MgO)</strong>, <strong>Alumina</strong>, and <strong>Potash</strong>. Highly base-saturated with high Cation Exchange Capacity (CEC).</li>\n                <li><strong>Nutrient Deficiencies:</strong> Poor in <strong>Nitrogen (N)</strong>, <strong>Phosphorus (P₂O₅)</strong>, and <strong>Humus</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Prime Crops:</strong> Globally famous as <strong>'Black Cotton Soil'</strong> for rain-fed cotton cultivation; also ideal for <em>soybean (MP), sugarcane, jowar, pigeon pea (tur), sunflower, and tobacco</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
