            "id": "m06",
            "title": "Arid, Saline-Alkaline (Usar/Reh) & Peaty/Marshy (Kari) Soils",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Arid (Desert) Soils</h3>\n<p><strong>Arid soils</strong> occupy approximately <strong>14.2 million hectares (about 4.32% of India's geographical area)</strong>, concentrated primarily in Western Rajasthan (Thar Desert), Southern Punjab, Haryana, and the Rann of Kachchh in Gujarat.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Physical Characteristics:</strong> Textures range from sandy to gravelly loams. The structure is loose, single-grained, and highly porous with high permeability and poor moisture-retention capacity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chemical Composition:</strong> Characterized by high concentrations of soluble salts and calcium. Soil reaction is alkaline (pH 7.5 to 8.5). They are severely deficient in nitrogen and organic humus, while phosphate content is normal to adequate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Kankar Horizon:</strong> In lower horizons, calcium carbonate content increases downward, forming hard, nodular <em>'kankar'</em> layers that impede downward percolation of water and root penetration.</li>\n  <li><strong>Agricultural Potential:</strong> When irrigated (such as via the <em>Indira Gandhi Canal / Rajasthan Canal</em>), these soils sustain drought-resistant crops including bajra (pearl millet), jowar, pulses, barley, mustard, and guar.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Saline and Alkaline Soils (Halomorphic Soils)</h3>\n<p>Known across India by varied vernacular names—<strong>Usar, Reh, Kallar, Thur, Rakar</strong> in Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, and Haryana; <strong>Chopan</strong> in Maharashtra; and <strong>Luni</strong> in Rajasthan.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Genesis & Causative Factors:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Dry Climatic Aridity:</em> Intense evapotranspiration brings dissolved subterranean salts to the surface via capillary action, depositing a white efflorescence (salt crust).</li>\n      <li><em>Over-irrigation in Canal Commands:</em> In the canal command areas of the Green Revolution (Indira Gandhi Canal, Western Yamuna Canal, Sharda Canal), seepage and improper drainage elevate the water table, dissolving subsurface salts and waterlogging the root zone.</li>\n      <li><em>Sea Inundation:</em> Coastal backwaters, mangrove fringes, and estuarine deltas (e.g., Sundarbans, Mahanadi delta, Rann of Kachchh) suffer marine tidal ingress.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Saline vs. Alkaline (Sodic) Distinction:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Saline Soils (Solonchak):</em> Dominated by neutral soluble salts (chlorides and sulphates of sodium, calcium, and magnesium). Electrical Conductivity (EC) &gt; 4 dS/m, Exchangeable Sodium Percentage (ESP) &lt; 15%, pH &lt; 8.5.</li>\n      <li><em>Alkaline/Sodic Soils (Solonetz):</em> Dominated by exchangeable sodium ($Na^+$) and sodium carbonates/bicarbonates ($Na_2CO_3, NaHCO_3$). ESP &gt; 15%, EC variable, pH &gt; 8.5 (often up to 10.0). Deflocculated structure renders them sticky when wet and rock-hard when dry.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Reclamation Techniques:</strong> Deep drainage tile installations, scraping off salt encrustations, flushing with abundant fresh water, application of <strong>gypsum ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$)</strong> or pyrites to replace exchangeable $Na^+$ with $Ca^{2+}$, and planting salt-tolerant cover crops like <em>Sesbania bispinosa (Dhaincha)</em> and <em>Karnal grass</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Peaty and Marshy Soils (Histosols & Gleysols)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Humid lowlands with poor natural drainage, especially the <strong>Kuttanad tract</strong> (Alappuzha and Kottayam districts of Kerala), Sundarbans delta (West Bengal), Mahanadi delta (Odisha), and coastal Almora/Nainital terai tracts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pedological Profile:</strong> Develop under anaerobic, waterlogged conditions where organic debris decomposes extremely slowly. They contain <strong>40% to 50% organic matter</strong>, resulting in a dark, heavy, and spongy soil mass.</li>\n  <li><strong>Kari Soils of Kerala:</strong> Locally termed <em>'Kari'</em> soils in Kuttanad, they are dark black, heavy peaty clays submerged during monsoons. Upon drying, oxidation of iron sulfides generates sulfuric acid, plummeting soil pH to <strong>3.0 to 4.5</strong> (acid sulfate soils). Specialized <em>'Pokkali'</em> rice cultivation occurs in these brackish coastal wetlands.</li>\n</ul>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Arid (Desert) soils are sandy, gravelly, low in organic humus and nitrogen, but rich in soluble salts and calcium carbonate (forming impermeable kankar sub-horizons).",
                "Saline and Alkaline soils (known locally as Reh, Kallar, Usar, Thur, Rakar, and Chopan) contain high concentrations of sodium, calcium, and magnesium salts due to poor drainage and excessive canal capillary action.",
                "Reclamation of alkaline/usar soils requires drainage improvement, addition of chemical amendments like gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) or pyrite, and salt-tolerant green manuring (Dhaincha).",
                "Peaty and Marshy soils (Kari soils in Kerala) develop in humid regions with high rainfall and waterlogging; they contain 40-50% organic matter and exhibit high acidity (pH 3.0 to 4.5)."
            ],
