            "id": "m07",
            "title": "Soil Degradation & Conservation in India (Water/Wind Erosion, Ravines & Bunding)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Dimensions of Soil Degradation in India</h3>\n<p>Soil degradation refers to the decline in soil quality and productive capacity caused by physical, chemical, and biological deterioration. In India, approximately <strong>120 to 147 million hectares</strong> suffer from degradation, with water erosion being the dominant agent.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Forms and Mechanics of Soil Erosion</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Water Erosion:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Splash (Raindrop) Erosion:</em> The kinetic impact of falling raindrops detaches aggregated soil particles, destroying soil structure and sealing macropores.</li>\n      <li><em>Sheet Erosion:</em> Uniform removal of a thin film of fertile topsoil over large non-vegetated expanses during heavy downpours. It is called <strong>'creeping death'</strong> because farmers rarely notice the gradual fertility depletion until crop yields collapse.</li>\n      <li><em>Rill Erosion:</em> Surface runoff concentrates into micro-channels or finger-like incisions (rills) a few centimeters deep. Rills can be smoothed out by normal tillage operations.</li>\n      <li><em>Gully Erosion:</em> As volume and velocity of runoff amplify, rills deepen and widen into steep-sided gullies that cannot be eradicated by ordinary cultivation. Gullies cut deep into subsoils, lowering water tables and dissecting fertile tablelands.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Chambal Ravines (Badland Topography):</strong>\n    <p>The lower Chambal Valley and its tributaries (Kalisindh, Parbati, Banas) across the border triangle of <strong>Madhya Pradesh (Bhind, Morena, Sheopur), Rajasthan (Dholpur, Sawai Madhopur), and Uttar Pradesh (Etawah, Agra)</strong> exhibit India's most catastrophic gully erosion. Deep ravines (up to 20 to 30 meters deep) have carved out extensive <em>'Badlands' (Khad)</em>, rendering thousands of square kilometers completely uncultivable.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Wind Erosion:</strong>\n    <p>Prevalent in arid Western Rajasthan, Southwestern Punjab, Haryana, and Northern Gujarat where sparse vegetation, loose sandy texture, and desiccating winds cause saltation, suspension, and surface creep of sand grains. This drives active dune migration and desertification along the Aravalli gaps.</p>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Soil Conservation Strategies in India</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Type of Measure</th>\n      <th>Techniques</th>\n      <th>Target Landscape / Mechanism</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Agronomic Practices</strong></td>\n      <td>Contour farming, Strip cropping, Mulching, Cover cropping, Crop rotation with legumes</td>\n      <td>Gentle slopes (&lt; 2%); reduces raindrop impact, enhances infiltration, maintains organic soil cover.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Mechanical / Engineering</strong></td>\n      <td>Contour bunding, Graded bunding, Bench terracing, Check dams, Gully plugging</td>\n      <td>Steep hill slopes (Western Ghats, Himalayas) and ravine zones; breaks slope length and dissipates runoff kinetic energy.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Wind Erosion Control</strong></td>\n      <td>Shelterbelts (tree rows), Windbreaks, Sand-dune stabilization via <em>Prosopis cineraria (Khejri)</em> &amp; grasses</td>\n      <td>Arid and semi-arid zones; perpendicular tree belts reduce surface wind velocities by up to 50-60%.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Integrated Watershed Management</strong></td>\n      <td>Ridge-to-valley treatment, percolation ponds, check dams, afforestation</td>\n      <td>Catchment basins; conserves both soil moisture and prevents downstream siltation of reservoirs.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Soil erosion in India is categorized into sheet erosion, rill erosion, and gully erosion (progressing to badland/ravine topography).",
                "The Chambal Ravines (Badlands) across Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, and Uttar Pradesh represent the most severe gully erosion landscape in India.",
                "Wind erosion predominates in arid Western Rajasthan, creating sand dunes, shifting barchans, and desertification fringes.",
                "Key engineering and agronomic conservation measures include contour bunding, graded terracing, check dams, shelterbelts, strip cropping, and mulching."
            ],
