            "title": "Forest Policy, Governance & State of Forest Report (ISFR)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Evolution of National Forest Policies</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>First Post-Independence Forest Policy (1952):</strong> Emphasized state-led timber exploitation, revenue generation, and designated vital, national, and village forests. Proposed the national target of <strong>33% forest cover</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>National Forest Policy of 1988:</strong> Marked a paradigm shift from commercial timber exploitation to <strong>environmental stability, ecological balance, and biodiversity conservation</strong>.\n    <ul>\n      <li>Reiterated the goal of maintaining <strong>33% of the total land area</strong> under forest or tree cover, and <strong>67% in fragile mountainous/hilly regions</strong> to prevent soil erosion and land degradation.</li>\n      <li>Pioneered <strong>Joint Forest Management (JFM)</strong>, institutionalizing village forest protection committees (VFCs) and tribal partnership in forest regeneration.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Constitutional Status:</strong> Under the <strong>42nd Amendment Act of 1976</strong>, 'Forests' and 'Protection of Wild Animals and Birds' were transferred from the State List (Entry 19) to the <strong>Concurrent List (Entry 17A &amp; 17B)</strong>. Article 48A (DPSP) and Article 51A(g) (Fundamental Duties) mandate environmental conservation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Key Statutory Frameworks</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Indian Forest Act, 1927:</strong> Categorizes forests into <em>Reserved Forests</em> (strictest protection; all acts prohibited unless permitted), <em>Protected Forests</em> (all acts permitted unless prohibited), and <em>Village Forests</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980:</strong> Prohibits the de-reservation of reserved forests or diversion of forest land for non-forest purposes without prior central government approval. Mandates <strong>Compensatory Afforestation Fund (CAMPA)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Forest Rights Act (FRA), 2006:</strong> Formally known as the <em>Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act</em>. Recognizes individual forest rights (IFR) and community forest rights (CFR), conferring legal tenure and empowerment to Gram Sabhas over minor forest produce.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. India State of Forest Report (ISFR) Insights</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Forest Survey of India (FSI)</strong>, headquartered at Dehradun, has been conducting biennial satellite-based forest assessments since 1987 (using IRS LISS-III sensor data):</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Parameter</th>\n      <th>Key Statistics &amp; Rankings</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Total Forest &amp; Tree Cover</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>8,09,537 sq km (24.62% of India's geographic area)</strong>.<br>Forest Cover: 7,13,789 sq km (21.71%); Tree Cover: 95,748 sq km (2.91%).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Top States by Absolute Forest Area</strong></td>\n      <td>1. <strong>Madhya Pradesh</strong> (77,493 sq km)<br>2. Arunachal Pradesh<br>3. Chhattisgarh<br>4. Odisha<br>5. Maharashtra</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Top States by Forest % of Area</strong></td>\n      <td>1. <strong>Mizoram</strong> (84.53%)<br>2. Arunachal Pradesh (79.33%)<br>3. Meghalaya (76.00%)<br>4. Manipur (74.34%)<br>5. Nagaland (73.90%)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>States Showing Maximum Forest Gain</strong></td>\n      <td>Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Odisha, Karnataka, and Jharkhand.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Mangrove Cover in India</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>4,992 sq km</strong>; Top states: West Bengal (42.3%), Gujarat (23.5%), Andaman &amp; Nicobar (12.3%).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n",
