            "id": "m14",
            "title": "Biodiversity Conservation: National Parks, Wildlife Sanctuaries & Biosphere Reserves",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Protected Area Network Framework</h3>\n<p>India is one of the world's <strong>17 megadiverse nations</strong>, harboring 7-8% of all recorded species. In situ conservation is operationalized through the <strong>Wildlife (Protection) Act (WPA), 1972</strong>:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color: #f2f2f2;\">\n      <th>Category</th>\n      <th>Legal Basis (WPA 1972)</th>\n      <th>Degree of Protection &amp; Permitted Activities</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>National Park</strong></td>\n      <td>Section 35</td>\n      <td><strong>Highest protection level:</strong> Total ban on livestock grazing, private land ownership, and extraction of any forest resource. Boundaries can only be altered upon recommendation of the National Board for Wildlife (NBWL).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Wildlife Sanctuary</strong></td>\n      <td>Section 18 / 26A</td>\n      <td>Specific floral/faunal focus. Certain limited human rights (such as controlled grazing of cattle, right to immovable property, collection of fuelwood) may be permitted by the Chief Wildlife Warden.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Conservation &amp; Community Reserves</strong></td>\n      <td>Section 36A / 36C (Added by 2002 Amendment)</td>\n      <td>Buffer zones or corridors connecting protected areas. Conservation reserves are established on government lands; Community reserves are established on private or community lands.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>2. The Biosphere Reserve Concept (UNESCO MAB Model)</h3>\n<p>Biosphere Reserves represent large terrestrial and coastal landscapes designated under <strong>UNESCO's Man and the Biosphere (MAB) Programme</strong> to reconcile biodiversity conservation with sustainable socio-economic development.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Three Concentric Zones:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Core Area (Strict Reserve):</em> Legally protected sovereign ecosystem (often National Parks/Sanctuaries) dedicated strictly to nature conservation without destructive human activities.</li>\n      <li><em>Buffer Zone:</em> Surrounds the core; used for compatible ecological activities including eco-tourism, environmental education, and applied ecological research.</li>\n      <li><em>Transition Zone (Zone of Cooperation):</em> Outermost periphery where local communities, managers, and scientists cooperate in sustainable resource management, agriculture, and forestry.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>India's 18 Biosphere Reserves:</strong> First created was the <strong>Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve (1986)</strong>. To date, <strong>12 out of 18</strong> are part of the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves: Nilgiri, Gulf of Mannar, Sundarbans, Nanda Devi, Nokrek, Pachmarhi, Similipal, Achanakmar-Amarkantak, Great Nicobar, Agasthyamala, Khangchendzonga, and Panna.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Flagship Species Conservation Projects</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Project Tiger (1973):</strong> Launched at Corbett National Park. Administered by the <strong>National Tiger Conservation Authority (NTCA)</strong>. India currently hosts <strong>55+ Tiger Reserves</strong> housing over 75% of the world's wild tigers (~3,682 tigers as per 2022 Census).</li>\n  <li><strong>Project Elephant (1992):</strong> Focuses on securing elephant corridors, mitigating human-elephant conflict (HEC), and establishing Elephant Reserves across 16 states.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ramsar Wetlands of International Importance:</strong> India has designated <strong>85+ Ramsar sites</strong> (including Sundarbans, Chilika Lake, Keoladeo Ghana, Loktak Lake, Vembanad Kol), safeguarding critical waterfowl flyways.</li>\n</ul>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "India's Protected Area (PA) network comprises 106 National Parks, 573+ Wildlife Sanctuaries, 115 Conservation Reserves, and 220 Community Reserves.",
                "Wildlife (Protection) Act (WPA), 1972 is the apex statutory legislation governing species protection, hunting prohibitions, and protected area declarations.",
                "There are 18 designated Biosphere Reserves in India; 12 are included in the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves under the Man and the Biosphere (MAB) Programme.",
                "Key flagship conservation initiatives include Project Tiger (1973, 55+ tiger reserves), Project Elephant (1992), and Project Snow Leopard (2009)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "National Parks have the highest legal protection (no human settlement or grazing allowed), whereas Wildlife Sanctuaries allow regulated traditional grazing and minor forest collection.",
                "Biosphere Reserves feature a three-tier zoning model: Core Zone (strictly protected), Buffer Zone (regulated research/eco-tourism), and Transition Zone (sustainable community agriculture).",
                "Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve (established in 1986) was India's first Biosphere Reserve and first UNESCO MAB site."
            ],
